From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MyNaai Dev <dev@mynaai.in>
Date: Wed, 5 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] Shop categories: one list for the admin panel, the website and the app

The shop classified products in the browser (a keyword guess over the name, plus
whatever free text the admin panel wrote into `shop_products.category`). Two
consequences the client reported:

  · the panel offered its own eight long labels, so a category the admin created
    was invisible on the website;
  · free text was mapped by guesswork — a bottle of herbal hair oil filed under
    "Hair Colour and Chemical Products" showed up as HAIR COLOUR.

This patch makes the list a table, with one endpoint behind it and one slug rule
shared by the panel, the website, the app and the bulk importer.

  POST /api/shop-categories/list           any signed-in user (app / website)
  POST /api/shop-public/categories/list    no token, rate-limited, 5 min cache
  POST /api/shop-categories/admin/list     admin: every row + productCount
  POST /api/shop-categories/admin/create   admin: { label, sortOrder? }
  POST /api/shop-categories/admin/update   admin: { id, label?, sortOrder?, isActive? }
  POST /api/shop-categories/admin/delete   admin: { id, reassignTo? }

Rules the code enforces (and the website already relies on):

  · the id is the label's slug, FIXED at creation. Renaming a label never
    changes the id, because every product stores the id. `create` refuses a
    duplicate id or a duplicate label.
  · `sort_order` accepts 0 — the first chip — and a new category is appended
    after the highest position.
  · deleting a category that has products is refused with the count in the
    message; with `reassignTo` the products move first (both the id and the old
    label spelling), then the row is deleted.
  · `is_active = false` hides it from the storefront filter but leaves it on the
    products already filed under it, and stays visible in the panel.
  · product create/update now validate `category` against this table and answer
    `Unknown category "x" — use one of the ids from the category list`. An empty
    value is still allowed (the shop falls back to inference).

Files

  new  src/utils/categorySlug.js                    the one slug rule
  new  src/database/models/shopCategory.model.js    shop_categories
  new  src/database/config/ensureShopSchema.js      idempotent DDL guard
  new  src/services/shopCategory.service.js         list / create / update /
                                                    delete / resolveCategoryId
  new  src/controller/shopCategory/*.js             five thin controllers
  new  src/routes/shopCategory.routes.js            mounted at /shop-categories
  new  migrations/2026-11-05-shop-categories-leads-bulk.sql
                                                    categories + lead columns +
                                                    bulk-import tables (one file,
                                                    three features, idempotent)
  mod  src/routes/routes.js                         mounts /shop-categories
  mod  src/routes/shopPublic.routes.js              adds /categories/list (POST + GET)
  mod  src/controller/shopProduct/adminCreateShopProduct.js
  mod  src/controller/shopProduct/adminUpdateShopProduct.js

The migration also re-files the eight legacy panel labels onto the seven real
categories (Hair Care Products -> hair-care, Shaving and Beard Products ->
beard-grooming, Facial and Skin Care Products -> skin-face, …) so the storefront
filter has seven chips instead of fifteen. Anything it does not recognise is left
untouched: the website still shows a chip for it, and the panel lists it under
"unmatched" so it can be re-filed by hand. Review before running:

  SELECT category, COUNT(*) FROM shop_products
   WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY category ORDER BY 2 DESC;

`src/database/config/ensureShopSchema.js` is the belt to that braces: it creates
the table and inserts the seven seeds once per process (no-op on a migrated
database), so the panel and the API answer even where nobody ran the SQL. It
deliberately does NOT rewrite existing catalogue rows — that stays in the
migration, where it can be reviewed first.

Apply order: this patch -> backend-2-booking-request-leads -> backend-3-shop-bulk-products.
This one stands alone; the next two import `ensureShopSchema`.

Verified: every file passes `node --check`; `src/routes/routes` loads and exposes
the five routes; the service was exercised against a stub ORM for create /
rename / hide / delete-refused / reassign-then-delete / resolve, including the
`sort_order: 0` case.
